    "content": "{\n  \"files\": [\n    {\n      \"content\": \"#include \\\"knn.h\\\"\\n#include <arm_neon.h>\\n\\n// Max-heap node structure\\ntypedef struct {\\n    float dist;\\n    char label;\\n} HeapNode;\\n\\nstatic inline void heap_swap(HeapNode *a, HeapNode *b) {\\n    HeapNode t = *a; *a = *b; *b = t;\\n}\\n\\n// Insert a single (dist,label) into the max-heap of size k.\\nstatic inline void heap_insert_or_replace(float d, char label,\\n                                          HeapNode * restrict heap, int *heap_size_ptr, int k) {\\n    int heap_size = *heap_size_ptr;\\n    if (heap_size < k) {\\n        // Insert into heap\\n        heap_size++;\\n        int idx = heap_size;\\n        heap[idx].dist = d;\\n        heap[idx].label = label;\\n        while (idx > 1 && heap[idx/2].dist < heap[idx].dist) {\\n            heap_swap(&heap[idx], &heap[idx/2]);\\n            idx /= 2;\\n        }\\n    } else if (d < heap[1].dist) {\\n        // Replace root\\n        heap[1].dist = d;\\n        heap[1].label = label;\\n        int idx = 1;\\n        while (1) {\\n            int largest = idx;\\n            int left = 2 * idx;\\n            int right = 2 * idx + 1;\\n            if (left <= heap_size && heap[left].dist > heap[largest].dist)\\n                largest = left;\\n            if (right <= heap_size && heap[right].dist > heap[largest].dist)\\n                largest = right;\\n            if (largest != idx) {\\n                heap_swap(&heap[idx], &heap[largest]);\\n                idx = largest;\\n            } else break;\\n        }\\n    }\\n    *heap_size_ptr = heap_size;\\n}\\n\\nchar knn(int n_groups, Group * groups, int k, Point to_evaluate) {\\n    // Allocate labels array (initialized with -1 sentinel)\\n    char * restrict labels = (char *) malloc(sizeof(char) * k);\\n    for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) labels[i] = -1;\\n\\n    // Max-heap of size k+1 (index 1..k)\\n    HeapNode * restrict heap = (HeapNode *) malloc(sizeof(HeapNode) * (k + 1));\\n    int heap_size = 0;\\n\\n    for (int i = 0; i < n_groups; i++) {\\n        Group g = groups[i];\\n        float * restrict xs = g.xs;\\n        float * restrict ys = g.ys;\\n        int len = g.length;\\n        char label = g.label;\\n\\n        // Prefetch next group's data (if any)\\n        if (i + 1 < n_groups) {\\n            __builtin_prefetch(groups[i+1].xs, 0, 3);\\n            __builtin_prefetch(groups[i+1].ys, 0, 3);\\n        }\\n\\n        // Broadcast query point coordinates once per group\\n        float32x4_t tx_vec = vdupq_n_f32(to_evaluate.x);\\n        float32x4_t ty_vec = vdupq_n_f32(to_evaluate.y);\\n\\n        // Process points in batches of 8 using NEON SIMD (vld1q_f32_x2)\\n        int j = 0;\\n        for (; j + 7 < len; j += 8) {\\n            // Prefetch next cache line ahead\\n            __builtin_prefetch(xs + j + 16, 0, 3);\\n            __builtin_prefetch(ys + j + 16, 0, 3);\\n\\n            float32x4_t x_vec_lo = vld1q_f32(xs + j);\\n            float32x4_t y_vec_lo = vld1q_f32(ys + j);\\n            float32x4_t x_vec_hi = vld1q_f32(xs + j + 4);\\n            float32x4_t y_vec_hi = vld1q_f32(ys + j + 4);\\n\\n            // First half (points j..j+3)\\n            float32x4_t dx0 = vsubq_f32(x_vec_lo, tx_vec);\\n            float32x4_t dy0 = vsubq_f32(y_vec_lo, ty_vec);\\n            float32x4_t dist_sq0 = vmlaq_f32(vmulq_f32(dx0, dx0), dy0, dy0);\\n\\n            // Second half (points j+4..j+7)\\n            float32x4_t dx1 = vsubq_f32(x_vec_hi, tx_vec);\\n            float32x4_t dy1 = vsubq_f32(y_vec_hi, ty_vec);\\n            float32x4_t dist_sq1 = vmlaq_f32(vmulq_f32(dx1, dx1), dy1, dy1);\\n\\n            // Store both halves consecutively using single x2 store\\n            float d[8];\\n            float32x4x2_t pair = {dist_sq0, dist_sq1};\\n            vst1q_f32_x2(d, pair);\\n\\n            for (int t = 0; t < 8; t++) {\\n                heap_insert_or_replace(d[t], label, heap, &heap_size, k);\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        // Remainder scalar loop (handles up to 7 leftover points)\\n        for (; j < len; j++) {\\n            Point p;\\n            p.x = xs[j];\\n            p.y = ys[j];\\n            float d = euclidean_distance_no_sqrt(to_evaluate, p);\\n            heap_insert_or_replace(d, label, heap, &heap_size, k);\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    // Copy labels from heap into labels array\\n    for (int i = 0; i < heap_size && i < k; i++) {\\n        labels[i] = heap[i+1].label;\\n    }\\n\\n    free(heap);\\n\\n    qsort(labels, k, sizeof(char), compare_for_sort);\\n\\n    char most_frequent = labels[0];\\n    int most_frequent_count = 1;\\n    int current_frequency = 1;\\n\\n    for (int i = 1; i < k; i++) {\\n        if (labels[i] != labels[i - 1]) {\\n            if (current_frequency > most_frequent_count) {\\n                most_frequent = labels[i - 1];\\n                most_frequent_count = current_frequency;\\n            }\\n            current_frequency = 1;\\n        } else {\\n            current_frequency++;\\n        }\\n\\n        if (i == k - 1 && current_frequency > most_frequent_count) {\\n            most_frequent = labels[i - 1];\\n            most_frequent_count = current_frequency;\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    free(labels);\\n    return most_frequent;\\n}\\n\\nint main() {\\n    int n_groups = parse_number_of_groups();\\n    \\n    Group * groups = (Group *) malloc(sizeof(Group) * n_groups);\\n\\n    for (int i = 0; i < n_groups; i++) {\\n        groups[i] = parse_next_group();\\n    }\\n\\n    int k = parse_k();\\n\\n    Point to_evaluate = parse_point();\\n\\n    printf(\\\"%c\\\", knn(n_groups, groups, k, to_evaluate));\\n}\\n\",\n      \"path\": \"src/knn.c\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"content\": \"#include <stdio.h>\\n#include <stdlib.h>\\n#include <string.h>\\n\\ntypedef struct {\\n    float x;\\n    float y;\\n} Point;\\n\\ntypedef struct {\\n    char label;\\n    int length;\\n    float * restrict xs;\\n    float * restrict ys;\\n} Group;\\n\\nvoid on_error() {\\n    printf(\\\"Invalid input file.\\\\n\\\");\\n    exit(1);\\n}\\n\\nint parse_number_of_groups() {\\n    int n;\\n    if (scanf(\\\" n_groups=%d \\\", &n) != 1) on_error();\\n    return n;\\n}\\n\\nPoint parse_point() {\\n    float x, y;\\n    if (scanf(\\\" (%f ,%f) \\\", &x, &y) != 2)  on_error();\\n    Point point;\\n    point.x = x;\\n    point.y = y;\\n    return point;\\n}\\n\\nGroup parse_next_group() {\\n    char label; \\n    int length;\\n    if (scanf(\\\" label=%c \\\", &label) != 1) on_error();\\n    if (scanf(\\\" length=%d \\\", &length) != 1) on_error();\\n\\n    Group group;\\n    group.label = label;\\n    group.length = length;\\n\\n    // Allocate xs and ys with 16-byte alignment for faster NEON loads\\n    void *xs_ptr, *ys_ptr;\\n    if (posix_memalign(&xs_ptr, 16, sizeof(float) * length) != 0 ||\\n        posix_memalign(&ys_ptr, 16, sizeof(float) * length) != 0) {\\n        on_error();\\n    }\\n    group.xs = (float *) xs_ptr;\\n    group.ys = (float *) ys_ptr;\\n\\n    for (int i = 0; i < length; i++) {\\n        Point p = parse_point();\\n        group.xs[i] = p.x;\\n        group.ys[i] = p.y;\\n    }\\n\\n    return group;\\n}\\n\\nint parse_k() {\\n    int k;\\n    if (scanf(\\\" k=%d \\\", &k) != 1) on_error();\\n    return k;\\n}\\n\\nstatic inline float euclidean_distance_no_sqrt(Point a, Point b) {\\n    return ((b.x - a.x) * (b.x - a.x)) + ((b.y - a.y) * (b.y - a.y));\\n}\\n\\nint compare_for_sort(const void *a, const void *b) {\\n  return *(char*)a - *(char*)b;\\n}\\n\",\n      \"path\": \"src/knn.h\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Adding __builtin_prefetch for upcoming cache lines at both group and point levels reduces memory latency. Using posix_memalign to allocate xs/ys arrays on 16-byte boundaries enables faster aligned NEON loads. Replacing two separate vst1q_f32 stores with a single vst1q_f32_x2 store reduces instruction count. Adding restrict qualifiers on heap and labels pointers eliminates aliasing concerns for the compiler. These changes maintain correctness while further improving SIMD throughput.\",\n  \"notes\": \"\",\n  \"sources_kept\": [],\n  \"transformations\": [\n    \"Added __builtin_prefetch for next group's xs/ys before processing current group\",\n    \"Added __builtin_prefetch inside batch loop to prefetch two cache lines ahead\",\n    \"Changed allocation of xs and ys in parse_next_group from malloc to posix_memalign with 16-byte alignment\",\n    \"Replaced separate vst1q_f32 stores for each half with a single vst1q_f32_x2 store using float32x4x2_t pair\",\n    \"Added restrict qualifier on heap and labels pointers in knn function and heap_insert_or_replace signature\"\n  ]\n}",
    "thinking": "We need to propose one bounded HPC source-code experiment. The goal is \"legal SIMD/vectorization and loop dependencies\". We are role B. Current sources: knn.c and knn.h. The parent selected from context is the most recent successful own-role experiment with ID 5f82e976c4f0428e (score ~0.9859). That parent used NEON SIMD batch size 8, restrict qualifiers, hoisting invariant point coordinates. Our new hypothesis should build on that but aim for further improvement.\n\nWe have automatic observations: baseline mean_seconds=2.09, selected_parent mean_seconds~0.02998. So we are already very fast (~30ms). Need to be careful not to break correctness or regress.\n\nPossible transformations:\n- Use vld4q_f32 (deinterleave) if data layout allows? But xs and ys are separate arrays, so cannot deinterleave easily.\n- Prefetch more aggressively: add __builtin_prefetch for next cache lines in the batch loop.\n- Reduce store/load overhead by accumulating distances into NEON registers and only storing when heap insertion needed? But we need individual distances per point. Could use pairwise reduction to find min distance among 8 points before inserting? No, we must insert all k nearest across groups.\n- Use vpaddq_f32 to sum squared differences more efficiently? Currently using vmlaq_f32(vmulq_f32(dx0,dx0), dy0,dy0) which is fine. Could use vfmaq_f32 (fused multiply-add) but that's same as vmlaq.\n- Use NEON for heap insertion? That might be complex and risky.\n- Reduce function call overhead: inline heap_insert_or_replace more aggressively or rewrite with manual loop unrolling.\n- Use aligned loads via __builtin_assume_aligned if we ensure alignment. But malloc may not guarantee 16-byte alignment; could use posix_memalign for xs/ys arrays in parse_next_group to enable vld1q_f32 without potential alignment faults (though NEON allows unaligned). Still, aligned loads can be faster.\n- Use restrict on heap pointer and labels array.\n\nGiven the parent already uses batch size 8 with vld1q_f32_x2. Could try increasing batch size further? But len may not be large enough; also register pressure. Another idea: use NEON to compute distances for two batches simultaneously (interleaving) but that might increase complexity.\n\nBetter approach: Use __builtin_prefetch for the next group's xs/ys arrays before entering inner loop, and prefetch within batch loop for future points. Also ensure heap insertion is optimized by using a sentinel or branchless min-heap? But we have max-heap; could convert to min-heap to avoid replacement logic? Actually current algorithm uses max-heap with root being largest distance among k nearest; if new dist < root, replace and sift down. That's standard.\n\nWe can also try to reduce the number of heap operations by first collecting distances in a local buffer and then batch-inserting? But that would require sorting or partial sort which may be slower.\n\nAnother idea: Use NEON to compute squared distances for 8 points at once, but instead of storing all 8 floats individually, we can use pairwise min reduction to find the smallest distance among those 8 and only insert if it's less than current heap root? But that would miss some points because multiple could be smaller. Not correct.\n\nGiven the time is already very low (~30ms), improvements may come from micro-optimizations: prefetch, alignment, reducing branch mispredictions in heap insertion (e.g., using a while loop with sentinel). Also note that the parent uses restrict on xs/ys pointers inside Group struct; we can also add restrict to heap pointer and labels.\n\nWe should propose one candidate. Let's design:\n\nHypothesis: \"Adding __builtin_prefetch for upcoming cache lines in both group-level and point-level loops reduces memory latency, while using aligned allocation (posix_memalign) for xs/ys arrays enables faster NEON loads. Additionally, hoisting the heap pointer into a local restrict variable and unrolling the inner store loop with explicit vst1q_f32_x2 reduces instruction overhead. These changes maintain correctness and further improve SIMD throughput.\"\n\nTransformations:\n- In parse_next_group: use posix_memalign to allocate xs/ys on 16-byte boundaries.\n- In knn function: add __builtin_prefetch for next group's data before processing current group, and prefetch ahead in batch loop (e.g., prefetch xs+j+8).\n- Use restrict qualifier on heap pointer and labels array.\n- Replace the scalar store loop with vst1q_f32_x2 to reduce store instructions? Actually we already use vst1q_f32 for each half; could combine into one x2 store but that requires contiguous memory. We have d[8] which is contiguous, so we can do float32x4x2_t dist_pair = {dist_sq0, dist_sq1}; vst2q_f32(d, dist_pair); That stores interleaved? No, vst2q_f32 would store alternating lanes from two vectors. We want sequential: first 4 then next 4. So better to keep separate stores or use vst1q_f32_x2 which is available in ARMv8.2+? Actually vld1q_f32_x2 loads two consecutive quadwords; similarly vst1q_f32_x2 stores two consecutive quadwords from a pair of vectors. That would be efficient: float32x4x2_t dist_pair = {dist_sq0, dist_sq1}; vst1q_f32_x2(d, dist_pair); This is available in ARM NEON with __ARM_FEATURE_SVE? Actually it's part of the Neon instruction set for AArch64 (vst1q_f32_x2). We can use that to store both halves at once.\n- Also hoist heap_size and k into local variables.\n\nWe must ensure correctness. The current code uses vld1q_f32 which works with unaligned; aligned may be faster but not required.\n\nLet's write the new knn.c content accordingly, preserving all other functions unchanged in knn.h (except maybe adding include for posix_memalign? Actually we need to modify parse_next_group in knn.h. We'll change allocation there.)\n\nWe must return only JSON with files array containing full contents of changed files. Since we are modifying both src/knn.c and src/knn.h, we need to provide complete content for each.\n\nAlso note: sources